            <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: concept: Use gestures and drawings to link daily routines to classroom activities that are performed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures or drawings to represent numbers (e.g., showing five objects in a drawing). Can use fingers to show numbers up to ten (e.g., holding up three fingers). Can clarify numbers when counting by asking a yes/no question supported with gestures (e.g., asking for repetition by using their fingers: 'Three or four?').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads the "Number Fingers Song" (to the tune of "Twinkle, Twinkle Little Star"). "One, two, three, four, five, fingers on my hand! Six, seven, eight, nine, ten, numbers I can understand!" Students sing and hold up fingers as they count. The teacher then shows flashcards of classroom items (pencil, book, crayon, block) and asks, "What is this?" to activate vocabulary. (Pragmatic: Counting aloud)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models counting objects by picking up 3 pencils and saying, "One, two, three pencils." While saying "three," the teacher holds up three fingers. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /k/ in 'count' and /f/ in 'fingers' by exaggerating the sounds. (Pronunciation: Initial /k/ in 'count' and /f/ in 'fingers')</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows 4 fingers) "Is this number five?" (No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up 2 crayons) "How many crayons?" (Two)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Real classroom objects (pencils, crayons, blocks, books), number flashcards (1-10), large drawing paper, markers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to use gestures and simple verbal explanations to communicate quantities of classroom items, thereby mediating information between objects and numerical representation. (Grammar: Quantity + nouns; Vocabulary: numbers 1-10, pencil, book, crayon, block)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the "Count & Sort Activity" (21st Century Skills Project). "Today, we will count and sort our classroom items! We will show numbers with our fingers!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a basket of mixed classroom items. "Look! Pencils, crayons, blocks!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher picks up three pencils, counts them aloud, and holds up three fingers. "One, two, three pencils. I have three pencils."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "How many pencils?" while holding up three fingers. (Expected student response: "Three!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher repeats with two blocks: "One, two blocks. I have two blocks." "How many blocks?" (Expected student response: "Two!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating numbers using gestures and simple sentences. (Grammar: 'How many X?', 'X fingers', 'Is this number Y?'; Vocabulary: count, show, point, numbers 1-10, hand, finger, pencil, book, crayon, marker, block)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Show Me the Number" (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher calls out a number (1-10) and an object (e.g., "Show me three pencils!"). Students respond by holding up the correct number of fingers. The teacher holds up corresponding object flashcards. (Learning Outcome: Can use fingers to show numbers up to ten; Pragmatic: Counting aloud)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Match the Count" (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> Students work in pairs. One student shows a number of fingers (e.g., five fingers). The partner points to a corresponding number of objects (e.g., five blocks) and says, "Five blocks!" The first student then asks, "Is this number five?" while showing the fingers again. The second student responds "Yes!" or "No, this is (correct number)." This practices clarifying numbers with gestures and yes/no questions. (Learning Outcome: Can clarify numbers when counting by asking a yes/no question supported with gestures; Sociolinguistic: Encouraging turn-taking)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Draw and Show" (Drawing mediation):</b> The teacher says, "Draw four red crayons." Students draw the crayons. After drawing, the teacher asks, "How many red crayons?" Students point to their drawing and hold up four fingers. This links drawings to numerical representation. (Learning Outcome: Can use gestures or drawings to represent numbers; Vocabulary: red, crayon)</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Count & Sort Activity" (21st Century Skills Project).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a mixed bag of classroom items (e.g., 5 pencils, 3 blue crayons, 2 yellow blocks).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to count and sort the items. As they sort, they must verbally mediate the count for each type of item to their group members and the teacher.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For example, a student sorts pencils. They count, "One, two, three, four, five pencils!" They then hold up five fingers and say, "Five pencils."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Another student sorts crayons. "One, two, three blue crayons!" They hold up three fingers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, asking questions like, "How many crayons?" or "Show me four blocks!" If a student is unsure, the teacher can ask a clarifying yes/no question with gestures: "Is this two or three blocks?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group presents their sorted items and the counts to the class, using their fingers and simple sentences. (Grammar: Quantity + nouns; Wh-questions; Yes/No questions; Pragmatic: Answering quantity questions; Sociolinguistic: Encouraging turn-taking and active listening)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Observe students during the "Count & Sort Activity" (Stage 4). The teacher uses an observation checklist to note individual and group participation and accuracy in mediating counts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., 'pencil', 'crayon', 'block', 'one', 'two', 'three', etc.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of gestures:</b> Accurately holding up fingers to represent numbers 1-10.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of communication:</b> Using simple sentences (e.g., "three pencils") to explain counts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarifying numbers:</b> Attempting to use yes/no questions or gestures to clarify.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participating in group counting and sorting, respecting turns.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up 3 fingers) "Can you show me three fingers?" (Students show 3 fingers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you count today?" (Students respond with object names or numbers, e.g., "Pencils!", "Five!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite thing to count today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show your friend how many pencils you counted?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate numbers and objects using gestures and simple language? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, group work).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students struggled with showing numbers with fingers or clarifying counts? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future number activities (e.g., more one-on-one modeling, simplified counting tasks, peer support)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did the "Count & Sort Activity" integrate the mediation skill and reinforce previously learned vocabulary and grammar?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit "All the Pencils!". The next lesson will transition to a new theme, possibly reviewing numbers in a different context or introducing new descriptive adjectives for objects.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>